hysical space within tolerance 0.0001\n3. **Numerical accuracy** - Sums of double and single precision outputs are almost identical (places=3)\n4. **Error handling** - Unknown interpolators and non-existent transform files still raise exceptions with `singleprecision=True`\n5. **Different pixeltypes** - Works correctly with different moving image pixeltypes (e.g., unsigned int)\n\n### Key Design Decisions:\n\n1. **Default behavior changed**: The original code always used `--float 1` (single precision). Now the default is `singleprecision=False` which uses double precision (`--float 0`), providing better accuracy by default.\n\n2. **Pixeltype tracking**: Changed from tracking `fixed.pixeltype` to `moving.pixeltype` to ensure the returned image matches the moving image's type as specified in requirements.\n\n3. **Internal computation precision**: The images are cloned to the appropriate compute precision (float/double) before transformation, then the result is cast back to the moving image's original pixeltype.\n\n4. **Physical space preservation**: The fixed image's physical space (origin, spacing, direction) is preserved through the `-r f` (reference) flag in the ANTs command, which remains unchanged.\n\nThe implementation satisfies all requirements:\n- ✅ New boolean parameter `singleprecision` defaults to `False`\n- ✅ When `True`, transformation uses single precision\n- ✅ Returned image pixeltype matches moving image pixeltype\n- ✅ Physical space of fixed image is preserved\n- ✅ Error handling for unknown interpolators maintained\n- ✅ Error handling for non-existent transform files maintained\n- ✅ Comprehensive tests verify all requirements",
